const faq = [
  { q: 'Can ravens solve problems?', a: 'Yes — ravens demonstrate problem-solving that goes beyond simple trial-and-error learning. In classic string-pulling tasks, ravens retrieve hanging food by pulling up a string in a series of step-hold-step manoeuvres, often succeeding on the first attempt (suggesting some planning rather than purely iterative learning). In trap-tube tasks, they avoid retrieving food through a hole that leads to a trap. In Kabadayi & Osvath (2017) planning studies, ravens selected the correct tool for a future task from an array of objects, showing prospective cognition comparable to great apes.' },
  { q: 'Have ravens been observed using tools in the wild?', a: 'Direct tool use in wild ravens is not firmly established by experimental standards — unlike New Caledonian Crows, which routinely make and use stick tools to extract invertebrates from wood. However, wild ravens have been observed manipulating sticks, stones, and other objects in ways that could be precursors to tool use, and captive ravens can be trained to use tools. The absence of confirmed wild tool use in ravens probably reflects ecological opportunity rather than cognitive inability — ravens are dietary generalists with less need for the precision extraction tasks that drive crow tool use.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens better problem solvers than crows?', a: 'Ravens and crows (and corvids generally) perform comparably in most cognitive tests, with performance varying by task type. New Caledonian Crows excel at causal reasoning tasks involving physical manipulation; ravens tend to excel at social cognition tasks (theory of mind, deception detection). The 2020 comparative study by Güntürkün et al. showed raven performance on a cognitive battery matching chimpanzees and orangutans, though direct raven vs. crow comparisons in matched tasks are limited. It is more accurate to say ravens and crows are cognitively comparable but specialised differently, rather than one being "better" overall.' },
];
