    <p class="qa-pillar">Behaviour · BEH·22</p>
    <h1>Raven Problem Solving: Insight, Tools, and Flexible Intelligence</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens demonstrate problem-solving through insight — selecting correct solutions without extended trial-and-error, using tools, understanding causality in physical tasks, and planning for future contingencies. Key experiments include string-pulling (1998+), trap-tube reasoning, metatool use, and prospective tool selection (Kabadayi & Osvath, 2017). Their problem-solving performance is comparable to that of great apes.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>String Pulling</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> String pulling — retrieving food attached to a hanging string by alternately pulling up the string and stepping on it — requires coordination and an understanding of the food-string connection. Heinrich and colleagues first described spontaneous string-pulling by naive ravens in the 1990s. Key findings:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Some ravens solved the string-pulling task on first presentation without prior exposure or trial-and-error.</li>
      <li>Ravens distinguished between connected and disconnected strings (not pulling unattached strings), demonstrating understanding of the physical relationship.</li>
      <li>Multi-string variants (food attached to only one of several parallel strings) were solved correctly, showing ravens could select the relevant string rather than pulling any available string.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>String-pulling has since been tested in dozens of bird and mammal species; corvids consistently outperform most species including many primates.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Trap-Tube and Causal Reasoning</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The trap-tube task presents a horizontal tube with food inside and a trap (hole) in the floor of the tube. The correct solution is to push the food away from the trap, not toward it. Ravens succeed at this task, and importantly, can transfer the solution principle to novel trap configurations — suggesting causal understanding of why the trap is a problem rather than simply memorised responses to specific stimuli.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>This causal reasoning extends to physical causality more broadly: ravens understand that an opaque container might hide food even when they did not observe the hiding; they predict the trajectory of moving objects; and they use "functional fixedness" — evaluating whether an object can serve a needed function.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Future Planning: The Kabadayi & Osvath Study</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The 2017 study by Kabadayi & Osvath in <em>Science</em> demonstrated that ravens can plan for future contingencies in a way comparable to great apes — and potentially exceeding some primate species.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <p>In the experiment, ravens were:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Shown a box that could be opened only with a specific tool.</li>
      <li>Presented with an array of objects including the correct tool but no box.</li>
      <li>After a delay (up to 17 hours), presented with the box — and they selected the correct tool from the array.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ravens also traded tokens for food rewards with a human partner — planning a future exchange. These results demonstrated that ravens (like great apes but unlike most other animals tested) can inhibit immediate reward to plan for a future contingency, and select appropriate objects for future use.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Insight vs Trial-and-Error</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> "Insight" in comparative cognition refers to solving novel problems without extended trial-and-error — suggesting some internal modelling of the problem rather than purely behavioural conditioning. Ravens show several hallmarks of insight learning:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Sudden solution (solving on the first or second attempt, not after many failures).</li>
      <li>Pausing before action (observing the problem before attempting).</li>
      <li>Transfer to novel variants of the problem.</li>
      <li>Resistance to distraction from salient but irrelevant features.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Not all raven problem-solving involves insight — ravens also learn through trial-and-error, social learning, and conditioning. The significance is that ravens possess the additional capacity for insight-based learning, which is rare in the animal kingdom.</p>
  </section>

